<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- PerformanceMarkingsImplementationPlanV2.md stage 10 rule 2: three parts
     whose key/time signature changes sometimes agree and sometimes don't.
     Bar 1: all C major, 4/4 (seed - never a change). Bar 2: Violin and
     Cello both move to D major (fifths 2) - the MAJORITY - Viola alone
     moves to F major (fifths -1) - the minority, so the majority collapses
     to one score row and only Viola gets its own part row (reported
     against files/ManyParts/dvorak-symphony-no-9-new-world-ii-largo.mxl,
     bar 46: a real file where 11 of 12 parts agree and 1 doesn't - a
     literal "score only when EVERY part agrees" made that one stray part
     spoil the row for the other 11). Bar 3: Violin and Cello both move to
     3/4 - majority - Viola alone moves to 2/4 - minority - same rule for
     time. Bar 4: all three move by the SAME +1-fifth change from wherever
     they already were (Violin fifths 2 -> 3, A major; Cello fifths 2 -> 3;
     Viola fifths -1 -> 0, C major) and back to 4/4 together - a unanimous
     change on both counts (agreement is judged by the SIZE of the key
     change, not the resulting absolute value - a transposing part's
     written key is permanently offset from a concert-pitch part's, so only
     a shared modulation's delta, never the new value itself, is ever the
     same across parts), reported as a single score row via the
     pre-stage-10 path. -->
